#: src/ui/settings_dialog.cpp:1629
msgid ""
"What a blend or opacity change does while several layers are selected (shift-"
"click them with the Move tool). \"Disabled\" edits one layer at a time; "
"\"All selected layers\" applies to every selected layer in one step; "
"\"Active layer only\" applies just to the active layer."
msgstr ""

#: src/ui/settings_dialog.cpp:1646
msgid "Freehand lasso"
msgstr ""

#: src/ui/settings_dialog.cpp:1649
msgid "Smooth the freehand lasso"
msgstr ""

#: src/ui/settings_dialog.cpp:1655
msgid ""
"On (the default) smooths the freehand lasso into a clean curve -- for both "
"the live preview and the committed selection -- which actually follows your "
"stroke more faithfully than the raw path, whose decimated samples read as a "
"jagged staircase. Off uses that raw path. The polygonal lasso is never "
"smoothed."
msgstr ""

#: src/ui/settings_dialog.cpp:1672
msgid "Preset dock"
msgstr ""

#: src/ui/settings_dialog.cpp:1674
msgid "Cards"
msgstr ""

#: src/ui/settings_dialog.cpp:1674 src/ui/tool.cpp:192
msgid "Grid"
msgstr "Mreža"

#: src/ui/settings_dialog.cpp:1692
msgid ""
"How the brush-preset dock lists the library. \"Cards\" (the default) gives "
"each preset a row carrying its tip and a stroke of that very brush, painted "
"by the real engine -- so you can see the mark it makes before you make it. "
"\"Grid\" packs the tips into a denser grid of tiles, for reaching a brush "
"you already know by sight."
msgstr ""

#: src/ui/settings_dialog.cpp:1719
msgid ""
"On (the default) keeps one shared tip size: resizing the brush resizes the "
"eraser and the other way round, so switching tools never changes the mark "
"you make. Off gives the eraser its own independent size."
msgstr ""

#: src/ui/settings_dialog.cpp:1737
msgid ""
"Off (the default) gives the eraser its own preset, kept apart from the "
"brush's, so reaching for one never re-points the other. Note that the two "
"preset lists do not overlap -- a preset belongs to the eraser because it "
"erases, and each tool is shown only its own -- so \"follow the brush\" can "
"at most follow it to a nib the eraser is never offered."
msgstr ""

